add(21,'The zero-sum lift torsor has sixteen elements','026(3)–(8);027(10)–(12)','unproved claim','provient canoniquement d’un torseur',
'The three nonzero mod-two vectors have four lifts each, and the zero-sum condition selects a sixteen-element torsor P_M from the sixty-four lift triples. Differences of zero-sum triples are precisely V_J(M_0), and evaluation u↦(u(x_i)) identifies that group with End(M_0). The matrix certificate constructs this torsor and its regular congruence-kernel action; Hua’s §5 gives the same construction, so the current result verifies the actual torsor underlying the registered model rather than only its cardinality.',
H+', §5, Un torseur canonique',L,'torseur canonique|somme nulle|V_J|P_M',['group:torsor'])
add(22,'The contracted-product subscript in formula(9)','027, opening formula(9)','reading question','P_M∧_{M_0^J}(M_0,pr_i)',
'The displayed subscript is M_0^J, although the immediately preceding definition makes P_M a torsor under its proper subgroup V_J(M_0). The typed reconstruction is extension of structure group along pr_i restricted to V_J(M_0), a surjective map to M_0. Hua’s §5 writes that restricted-group interpretation; it identifies the required mathematical map but cannot replace the frozen subscript.',
H+', §5, first torsor formulas',L,'pr_i|V_J|produit contracté',['proof:contracted-product-type'])
add(23,'A canonical decomposition, stronger than a dimension count','027(13)–028(17)','unproved claim','M_0 et F_2^{ω} sont orthogonaux',
'The three reflections σ_i, together with zero, form the canonical copy of M_0 in End(M_0), while the two order-three automorphisms ρ,ρ′ span the complementary copy of F_2^ω and satisfy ρ+ρ′=id. The trace pairing makes the summands orthogonal and gives sl(M_0)=M_0⊕F_2·id; all sixteen matrices and all six conjugating automorphisms were checked. Hua’s §5 restates the decomposition, and LM_140-2_notes.md, LM_P057api R17 at scan569, already supplies the related trace-zero quotient identification; the added content here is the canonical summand and its use in splitting P_M into I_M and S_M.',
H+', §5; LM_140-2_text.tex,568–569(68); LM_140-2_notes.md, LM_P057api R17',L+'; bridge B5; LM2 C.d1 remains an unreviewed all-level claim','sigma_i|rho|orthogonaux|R17|569|mathfrak.*sl',['group:End-decomposition','congruence:equivariance'])
add(24,'Typing the reduction-kernel map','028, final paragraph after(19)','reading question','u↦id_M+2u',
'For u∈End_F2(M_0), choose any Λ-linear lift ũ and set u↦id_M+2ũ, which is independent of the lift and reduces to id_(M_0). The frozen prose instead describes a composite into End_F2(M_0) while writing an endomorphism of M, so its source and target need this interpretation. Hua’s §5 explicitly supplies the lift convention, and the matrix calculation verifies the resulting sixteen-element kernel; the diplomatic wording remains unchanged.',
H+', §5, footnote following the exact kernel sequence',L,'relèvement|id_M|endomorphisme|réduction',['group:reduction-kernel'])
add(25,'The canonical groupoid theorem and its actual proof scope','028(18)–029(21)','unproved claim','une équivalence can. de groupoïdes',
'The natural construction sends M to its mod-two space and the two quotient torsors of P_M, hence to (I,Q,k), with k:ω(I)≃C(Q). On the standard module the certificate computes these quotient actions directly, finds a faithful ninety-six-element image, and identifies it with the full automorphism group of the target triple; since both groupoids of ordinary finite objects are connected, this proves equivalence in that setting. The construction is natural, whereas a chosen identification with a standard object is not, and extension to locally constant families additionally uses descent. This independently verifies the scope of Hua’s registered finding 64-gl2-z4-combinatorial-model without treating its earlier AI-assisted interpretation as a reviewed theorem or claiming a historical priority.',
H+', §5, Théorème/Corollaire; notes/RESULTS_STATE.md, Hua finding',L,'64-gl2-z4-combinatorial-model|triplets|équivalence canonique|Corollaire',['group:canonical-action','proof:connected-groupoids'])
add(26,'The fibre product keeps the correct character','029(22);030(23);038, opening diagram','unproved claim','diagramme cartésien canonique',
'The actual group is S4×_{C2}D8, where the maps are the sign on I and the codiagonal character χ_C of the square, giving 24·8/2=96 elements. Its projection to D8 has kernel A4, while its projection to S4 has kernel F_2^ω; these are different quotients and neither projection should be replaced by determinant. The computed action realizes this precise fibre product, agreeing with Hua’s §5 and providing finite bookkeeping relevant to LM2 C.r3’s genuine-versus-outer-action distinction, without checking that earlier arithmetic claim.',
H+', §5, diagramme cartésien; LM2_CONGRUENCE_ROWS.md, C.r3',L+'; LM2 C.r3 carried as previous-run background','produit fibré|chi_C|C.r3|genuine|outer',['group:fibre-product'])
add(27,'Derived subgroups and the determinant line','030, Proposition;031(25)–(27);032, right brace','unproved claim','le quotient (SL_Λ(M))_ab est cyclique d’ordre 4',
'Exact commutator closure gives SL2(Λ)′≃A4 of order12 and GL2(Λ)′≃A4×{±I} of order24, hence abelianizations C4 and C2×C2 respectively. Conjugation by GL2(Λ) on the cyclic SL abelianization acts through determinant, supporting the determinant-line interpretation in the right brace of032 rather than an arbitrarily trivialized C4. Hua’s §5 gives the same structural assertions; the certificate checks their finite content, including the two central involutions and the distinct commutator quotients.',
H+', §5, Sous-groupes distingués et quotients',L,'commutateurs|cyclique d.ordre|abélianisé|bigwedge.*Lambda',['group:derived'])
add(28,'What the five cartesian squares and chosen splittings say','032(28), diagram and final paragraphs','unproved claim','les cinq carrés ... sont cartésiens',
'The five squares amount to explicit subgroup intersections, all of which the certificate checks together with their displayed indices. The semidirect decompositions of GL2(Λ) require a trivialization of P_M, exactly as the final paragraph says, while the stated direct product applies to ker(sg·det) after that choice. Hua’s §5 interprets the final GL product as semidirect; its footnote’s description of a written direct-product sign differs from the frozen middle dot and is a reading question, not permission to replace that sign.',
H+', §5, diagram and product footnote',L,'cinq carrés|cartésiens|trivialisation|produit direct|demi',['group:lattice'])
add(29,'The split GL reduction does not imply a split SL reduction','028, marginal semidirect-product calculation;032','own idea','produit 1/2 direct',
'The reduction GL2(Λ)→GL2(F2) splits after choosing the torsor point, but SL2(Λ)→SL2(F2) does not: every square-one element of SL2(Λ) reduces to the identity, so a transposition in S3 has no involutory lift. This gives a useful limit on transferring the displayed GL decomposition to the determinant-one subgroup, rather than contradicting the manuscript’s final determinant condition. Confidence is high because the certificate exhausts all forty-eight SL matrices; Hua’s determinant formula and LM_140-2_text.tex,568–569(68)–(71), are the relevant comparisons.',
H+', §5, determinant condition; LM_140-2_text.tex,568–569',L+'; bridge B5 only at levels2 and4','SL.*GL|déterminant|produit.*direct|568|569',['group:SL-nonsplit'])
add(30,'The projective line gives an octahedron with forty-eight automorphisms','033(29)–034(33)','unproved claim','points à val. dans Λ ... de la droite projective',
'The twelve primitive vectors modulo multiplication by ±1 give six vertices, with the three mod-two directions as antipodal pairs, twelve edges, and eight faces. Enumeration verifies that the action kernel is exactly {±I} and the image is the full forty-eight-element octahedral group, whose determinant-one subgroup is the rotational S4. Hua’s §6 and LM_140-2_text.tex,569(70)–(71), supply the corresponding group interpretation, but the finite calculation does not construct the modular curve or the Fermat-conic map proposed in that older passage.',
H+', §6; LM_140-2_text.tex,569–570',L+'; bridge B5; older geometric assertion remains unchecked here','octaèdre|Fermat|PSL|70|71',['group:octahedron'])
add(31,'Faces are the zero-sum torsor modulo its central involution','035(34)–(38)','unproved claim','F(Δ_M)≃P_M/Z',
'A zero-sum lift triple determines one vertex over each mod-two direction, hence a face, and the certificate verifies that every face has exactly two such triples, differing by simultaneous negation. This proves the claimed quotient of the sixteen-element torsor by Z and the induced identification End(M_0)/F_2·id≃F_2^J. Hua’s §6 gives the same map; its relevance is that the lost scalar lift is visible before the later reconstruction question, not only in an abstract count of automorphisms.',
H+', §6, torseur des faces',L,'faces|P_M/Z|F_2.*J|négation',['group:face-torsor'])
add(32,'Three named two-sets encode only two independent characters','036(40)–(44);039(50)','unproved claim','les trois caractères non triviaux',
'With sg the sign of the permutation on the three nonzero mod-two directions, the manuscript’s two-sets carry the characters ω_Δ=sg, Or(Δ)=det, and C_Δ=sg·det. The signed action on all six vertices verifies these identities, so the three torsors satisfy one contracted-product relation and do not provide three independent binary choices. Hua’s §6 agrees, while LM2 C.r1–C.r3 concern an adelic multiplier and outer actions and cannot be identified with these finite characters until a specific pullback homomorphism is supplied.',
H+', §6, three torsors; LM2_CONGRUENCE_ROWS.md, C.r1–C.r3',L+'; LM2 C.r1–C.r3 carried as unreviewed background','trois caractères|couleurs|orientation|C.r1|C.r2|C.r3',['group:characters'])
add(33,'Twisting away orientation does not choose an orientation of the original octahedron','036, final line;037(45)–(46)','unpursued implication','l’octaèdre orienté canoniquement associé',
'The contracted product Or(Δ)∧_{C2}Δ has trivial orientation torsor because the antipodism reverses spatial orientation; twisting it back by Or(Δ) recovers Δ. This is a canonical construction of a new oriented object, not a preferred orientation of the original one. Hua’s §6 uses the same twist, and the checked character relation in C64-N32 supplies the equivariance calculation needed to distinguish it from the convention choice on007.',
H+', §6, octaèdre orienté associé; current007',L,'octaèdre orienté|Or.*Delta|torsion|antipodisme',['proof:orientation-twist'])
add(34,'The linear lifting problem is explicitly left as a problem','037(47)–038, bracketed discussion','self-flagged','Il importe maintenant de bien comprendre la structure d’extension',
'The manuscript asks which additional datum lifts an octahedron torsor through 1→{±1}→GL2(Λ)→Aut_oct(Δ)→1, then reduces it on038 to lifting the prescribed diagonal and codiagonal two-sets to a square. This is a precise reduction of the question, but scans002–039 do not give an obstruction class or a general criterion for its vanishing. Hua’s §6 restates the same reduction; the projective-to-linear comparison in LM_140-3_text.tex,176–178(19)–(21), is a separate arithmetic problem whose scan177 explicitly postpones a check.',
H+', §6, lifting question; LM_140-3_text.tex,176–178',L+'; LM2 C.r3 gives a related typing issue, not a solution','remonter|relever|structure d.extension|176|177|178',['group:extension','scope:LM-extension'])
add(35,'A cohomological formulation of the square obstruction','037–039, extending the stated lifting question','own idea','remonter un torseur',
'For locally constant torsors over a specified base or topos B, with constant central kernel C2, put c=[C_Q]=[ω_I] and d=[D_Q]=[C_Δ]=c+μ, where μ=[Or(Δ)]; the lifting obstruction is c∪d=c∪(c+μ) in H²(B,C2). Choosing involutory lifts of the two coordinate generators gives the central cocycle c(g)d(h), which the certificate verifies on all2304 pairs in the forty-eight-element quotient; this is also consistent with the classical dihedral obstruction in Ledet(2001,§0.4), after changing rotation/reflection coordinates. When this class vanishes, lifts together with an identification of their quotient form, up to isomorphism, a torsor under H¹(B,C2), with central automorphisms H⁰(B,C2). Confidence is high for this explicitly scoped deduction from the checked cocycle, with no novelty or manuscript-attribution claim and no assertion that the required geometric or arithmetic base has been constructed here.',
H+', §6, D4/Z lifting; LM_140-3_text.tex,176–178; Ledet(2001,§0.4)',L+'; LM2 C.r3 comparison only; no resolution of EXIT_TOWER E.24','torseur|obstruction|relever|D_4|D4|E.24',['group:cocycle','proof:central-lift'])
add(36,'An explicit action that does not lift','037–038, central extension','own idea','donnée supplémentaire',
'The class of T=[[0,−1],[1,0]] in GL2(Λ)/{±I} has order two, while both lifts ±T square to −I. Sending the generator of C2 to this quotient class therefore gives an octahedral action with no C2-equivariant linear lift, proving the central extension is nonsplit. This does not say that an individual octahedron over the category of sets lacks some module representative; it shows that a coherent choice compatible with this action is impossible. Confidence is high because the two possible lifts and their squares are explicit and independently checked, refining Hua’s lifting discussion without claiming a counterexample to its groupoid theorem.',
H+', §6, lifting discussion; LM_140-3_text.tex,176–178',L+'; relevant finite analogy for LM2 C.r3','extension centrale|remonter|torseur|antipodisme',['group:nonliftable-action'])
add(37,'The prime on the octahedral automorphism group in(47)','037(47), lower identification; compare034(33) and037(48)','reading question','Aut_oct(Δ_M)′',
'The frozen label in(47) has a prime, although the adjoining product Aut_oct^+(Δ_M)×{1,a_Δ} has order48 and the valid quotient GL(M)/{±I} has that same order. If the prime meant a further quotient by the octahedral centre, its order would instead be24; formula(48) and Hua’s §6 use the unprimed full octahedral group. This is a notation or reading question only, and no post-context alteration has been made.',
H+', §6, projective quotient; current034 and037',L,'Aut.*oct|centre|quotient|GL.*prime',['group:octahedron'])
add(38,'What projectivization remembers and forgets','039, recap(49) and paragraph before(50)','unpursued implication','mais non pas Q_M',
'The projective line determines Δ_M and its four-set of opposite face pairs I_M, while the square Q_M retains the central linear lifting information. The loss is already detected on automorphisms: −I acts trivially on Δ_M but as the nontrivial antipodism on the square, so the full construction cannot descend unchanged through projectivization. Hua’s §6 makes the same distinction, and the finite certificate verifies it; this bears on LM2 C.r3’s typing discipline but does not recover the arithmetic extension in its separate context.',
H+', §6, Récapitulation; LM2_CONGRUENCE_ROWS.md,C.r3',L+'; LM2 C.r3 carried as background','mais non pas|Q_M|projective|C.r3',['group:projective-loss'])
